desktop-shell-window-snap(1)

# NAME

desktop-shell-window-snap - Windows-style window snapping from the keyboard, and optional snapping title bars

# SYNOPSIS

*desktop-shell-window-snap* [*-c* _FILE_] [*-s* _FILE_]

# DESCRIPTION

*desktop-shell-window-snap* snaps the focused window as Windows does with
the Windows key and the arrows (halves, quarters, maximize, restore; see
KEYBOARD), and removes tab groups that hold nothing but another group.

By default sway's title bars are left alone: dragging one moves the window
natively. The installed
_@sysconfdir@/sway/config.d/60-desktop-shell-window-snap.conf_ only starts
the program and defines the mode used during optional title-bar drags.

# OPTIONAL SNAPPING TITLE BARS

With these two lines in the sway configuration, title bars behave as on
Windows, without any modifier key:

```
bindsym button1 mark --add _window-snap-target, nop desktop-shell-window-snap press
bindsym --release button1 nop desktop-shell-window-snap release
```

- A click focuses the window, switching to its tab.
- A double click maximizes the window over the whole workspace, as a
  floating window, or restores it to where it was.
- Dragging the title bar shows where the window will land, and puts it there
  on release: near a screen side it fills that half, in a corner that
  quarter, at the top edge the whole workspace; over another tiled window it
  goes back into the tiling next to it, on the side nearest the pointer, or
  over that window's title bar as a tab with it; anywhere else a floating
  window simply moves. A maximized or snapped window dragged away gets its
  former size back. Escape or the right button cancels.

To follow the drag, the program moves the pointer by a pixel and presses
the button through sway's seat commands, which can feel less direct than
sway's own move. It never takes the keyboard; it switches to the
*desktop-shell-window-snap* mode only while a window is dragged, so that
Escape cancels the drag.

Windows that end up floating get a border of *floating-border-width*, tiled
ones *tiled-border-width*. After a package upgrade, *swaymsg reload*
restarts the program from the new file.

# KEYBOARD

The program also snaps the focused window when a binding runs
*nop desktop-shell-window-snap* _WORD_. The words *left*, *right*, *up* and
*down* step as Windows does with Super and the arrow keys: *left* then *up*
gives the top left quarter, *up* maximizes a free window, *down* restores a
maximized one. *left-half*, *right-half*, *maximize* and *restore* go
straight to that state. For example:

```
bindsym Mod4+Left  nop desktop-shell-window-snap left
bindsym Mod4+Right nop desktop-shell-window-snap right
bindsym Mod4+Up    nop desktop-shell-window-snap up
bindsym Mod4+Down  nop desktop-shell-window-snap down
bindsym Mod4+Shift+Left  nop desktop-shell-window-snap monitor-left
bindsym Mod4+Shift+Right nop desktop-shell-window-snap monitor-right
```

On a window against an edge, *left* or *right* carries it to the monitor on
that side, as the mirrored shape there; on the last monitor it stays.
*monitor-left* and *monitor-right* move the window to the monitor on that
side, keeping its snapped shape, or its size and relative place. *down* on a
free window does nothing, as there is no minimizing. A fullscreen window
leaves fullscreen first. A snapped window moved by hand counts as free
again. Each word waits for a fresh window tree, as floating windows move
without the compositor reporting it; words typed quickly are handled in
turn, and a word that changes nothing sends no command.

# NEW WINDOWS AND WINDOWS OUT OF REACH

A floating window that opens bigger than its monitor is shrunk to fit
(*fit-new-windows*); one that opens exactly over another moves 32 pixels
down and right (*cascade-new-windows*). This happens once, 150 ms after the
window opens, when its size has settled. When monitors change, every
floating window not fully on a monitor is brought fully onto the nearest
one; after any other key binding, only a window whose title bar cannot be
reached is (*bring-back-off-screen-windows*).

# OPTIONS

*-h, --help*
	Show the help message and exit.

*-c, --config* _FILE_
	Read settings from _FILE_ instead of the usual places.

*-s, --style* _FILE_
	Load _FILE_ instead of the shipped window-snap.css.

# SETTINGS

Read from _$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/desktop-shell-components/window-snap.ini_,
else _/etc/xdg/desktop-shell-components/window-snap.ini_. All are optional;
*swaymsg reload* applies changes.

*[snap]*
	*enabled* = _true_|_false_++
	*edge-zone-size* = _2-400_ pixels (24)++
	*drag-threshold* = _1-100_ pixels (8)++
	*floating-border-width* = _0-50_ (6)++
	*tiled-border-width* = _0-50_ (2)

*[preview]*
	*opacity* = _0-100_ percent (30)++
	*show-labels* = _true_|_false_

*[appearance]*
	*follow-sway-bar* = _true_|_false_

# SEE ALSO

*sway*(5), *desktop-shell-window-context-menu*(1)
